WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
We want to catch the more specific exception first.

00:06.000 --> 00:11.000
So I'm going to make a little function here.

00:11.000 --> 00:14.000
And I'm going to call it division.

00:14.000 --> 00:19.000
And it's going to take the number that to divide by.

00:19.000 --> 00:22.000
So I'm going to just call that num.

00:22.000 --> 00:28.000
And I'm going to say, try.

00:28.000 --> 00:31.000
Result equals.

00:31.000 --> 00:35.000
Let me make sure my indentation is correct here.

00:35.000 --> 00:43.000
Result is going to equal to the value of pi divided by the user input number.

00:43.000 --> 00:49.000
We're going to accept on arithmetic error.

00:50.000 --> 00:57.000
And print out that we had a general math error.

00:57.000 --> 01:05.000
Otherwise, I'm also going to accept on a special type of exception called the zero division error.

01:05.000 --> 01:08.000
Why might this come up?

01:08.000 --> 01:13.000
That means you're going to divide by zero and zero.

01:13.000 --> 01:17.000
So if the number is zero, yeah.

01:17.000 --> 01:21.000
I can say I divide by zero error.

01:21.000 --> 01:25.000
I think you're going to have problems because the arithmetic is still wrong.

01:25.000 --> 01:27.000
Oh, you missed the first item.

01:27.000 --> 01:29.000
Oh, thank you.

01:29.000 --> 01:35.000
When we just put that back in real quickly.

01:35.000 --> 01:39.000
So result equals 3.14 divided by num.

01:39.000 --> 01:43.000
And accept our arithmetic.

01:43.000 --> 01:44.000
Got that right.

01:44.000 --> 01:47.000
Yeah, error.

01:47.000 --> 01:52.000
I'm going to print out math error.

01:52.000 --> 02:00.000
And then I'm going to accept on zero division error.

02:00.000 --> 02:07.000
And I'm going to print zero division.

02:07.000 --> 02:09.000
Okay.

02:09.000 --> 02:15.000
If I try to do division by zero, we're going to see something a little bit unexpected.

02:15.000 --> 02:17.000
I'm going to get a math error.

02:17.000 --> 02:23.000
And that's because the zero division error is the more specific error.

02:23.000 --> 02:26.000
And arithmetic error is the more general one.

02:26.000 --> 02:34.000
So by catching the more general exception first, I swallowed the more helpful error message.

02:35.000 --> 02:43.000
And to figure out this exception hierarchy, you can look at the Python docs page.

02:43.000 --> 02:46.000
And check it out.

02:46.000 --> 02:47.000
There's a lot there.

02:47.000 --> 02:51.000
The link is in the course website.

02:51.000 --> 02:57.000
In general, it's pretty bad to catch exception in your code.

02:57.000 --> 03:02.000
Because let's say I was using the GitHub API.

03:02.000 --> 03:07.000
And I caught exception when I tried to use it.

03:07.000 --> 03:14.000
I just completely lost context of what the general exception was.

03:14.000 --> 03:20.000
And now I can't really change the flow of my program by what I can do with it.

03:20.000 --> 03:35.000
The absolute worst thing that you can do is try some code, accept on exception.

03:35.000 --> 03:40.000
And then do nothing.

03:40.000 --> 03:44.000
Everything went fine, according to my code, right?

03:45.000 --> 03:49.000
So this is called swallowing the exception.

03:49.000 --> 03:52.000
You should at least try to log it or do something.

03:52.000 --> 03:57.000
Maybe page your ops guy, depending on the severity of the error.

03:57.000 --> 04:02.000
But by doing this, you'll really lose a lot of context.

04:02.000 --> 04:06.000
And again, generally don't catch basic exception.

04:06.000 --> 04:09.000
Or now generally, but definitely don't do that.

04:09.000 --> 04:10.000
Whatever.

04:10.000 --> 04:12.000
But it's even the worst form of this.

